The ICE tabular method is a powerful method to obtain the equilibrium concentration of all species involved in an equilibrium. Here we lay the foundations of the steps needed to obtain those quantities. First we, set up the ICE table for the equilibrium, indicating the initial concentrations (or pressures) of the mixture to then obtain the expression of $K$ (sometimes $K_p$ others $K_c$ or simply $K$) in terms of the extent $x$ to then compute the polynomial associated $P(x)$. Now we obtain the roots of $P(x)$ and plug them back into the ICE chart. Finally, at this point, the last line of the table will give the equilibrium concentrations. Figure \ref{fig:Figure-Equilibrium-method} visally explains this method.
